 ## Overview
-This method converts a free-form object represented by a Catmull-Clark subdivision surface (CCSS) into a voxel grid by recursively subdividing the 2D parameter space of surface patches and sampling 3D points, rather than subdividing 3D object space [S1]. It ensures leak-free results for closed objects when a 3D flooding operation is performed by guaranteeing that voxels generated from a single patch satisfy specific N-adjacency properties [S1]. Unlike 3D space subdivision techniques which are computationally expensive due to distance computations between 3D points, this method performs subdivision in 2D parameter space, avoiding expensive 3D distance computations [S1].
+This method converts a free-form object represented by a Catmull-Clark subdivision surface (CCSS) into a voxel grid by recursively subdividing the 2D parameter space of surface patches and sampling 3D points, rather than subdividing 3D object space [S1]. It guarantees leak-free results for closed objects when a 3D flooding operation is performed by ensuring shared boundaries between patches or subpatches map to the same voxel and by recursively subdividing until the generated voxels satisfy specific N-adjacency conditions [S1]. Unlike 3D space subdivision techniques which are computationally expensive due to distance computations between 3D points, this method performs subdivision in 2D parameter space, avoiding expensive 3D distance computations [S1].
 
